C EXAMPLE - LOCAL POINTERS AND FUNCTION CALLS

PURPOSE: Confirm that a function updates a caller local through int*.
Write /tmp/set.c via write_file:

  void set(int *p) {
      *p = 65;
      return;
  }
  int main() {
      int n = 0;
      set(&n);
      if (n == 65) {
          write(1, "Y\n", 2);
      } else {
          write(1, "N\n", 2);
      }
      return 0;
  }

Run: cc /tmp/set.c, then as /tmp/set.s, then /tmp/a.out.
Expected stdout: Y

No global variables or arbitrary C library functions. A function
accepts at most four parameters; local frame is fixed at 256 bytes.
Pointers are not bounds-checked; never use fabricated hardware
addresses just to demonstrate pointer arithmetic.
For limits see /man/languages/c and /man/limits.
